For the kinetic energy, $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{dT}{dt}
 &=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12m\sum_i v_i^2\right) \\
 &=\sum_i m v_i\frac{dv_i}{dt}
 =\sum_i m v_i a_i.
 \end{aligned}$ The potential changes because the particle moves through space. The multivariable chain rule gives $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{dU}{dt}
 &=\sum_i\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_i}
             \frac{dx_i}{dt} \\
 &=\sum_i\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_i}v_i.
 \end{aligned}$ Adding the two derivatives, $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{dE}{dt}
 &=\sum_i v_i\left(ma_i+\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_i}\right) \\
 &=\sum_i v_i(F_i-F_i)=0.
 \end{aligned}$ The first $F_i$ is Newton’s $ma_i$; the second comes from $\partial_iU=-F_i$. The cancellation is exact.

