p.src:
For one particle moving in one dimension, the unknown path is $x(t)$. Since kinetic and potential energy have dominated the discussion, a natural first guess is $\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(T+U)\,dt.$ That is the total energy integrated over time, and it is the wrong guess. The correct action is $\begin{aligned}
 A[x]
 &=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(T-U)\,dt \\
 &=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}
   \left[\frac12m\dot x^{\,2}-U(x)\right]dt.
 \end{aligned}$ The difference $L(x,\dot x)=T-U$ is the Lagrangian, and the action is its integral over time.

